---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { HAS_FLAGS, IS_FLAGS, KEYS, SORT_FIELDS, UNITS } from "../search/keys";
const cardKeys = KEYS.filter((k) => k.scope === "card");
const printingKeys = KEYS.filter((k) => k.scope === "printing");
const numericKeys = KEYS.filter((k) => k.kind === "number").map((k) => k.aliases[0]);
const dateKey = KEYS.find((k) => k.kind === "date")!.aliases[0];
const alias = (name: string) => KEYS.find((k) => k.name === name)!.aliases[0];
const q = (s: string) => `/search?q=${encodeURIComponent(s)}`;
/** One list of sections, so the index and the headings cannot drift
 * apart: the index is built from this, and each section takes its id
 * and heading from the same entry. A mistyped key fails the build. */
const S = {
  words: { id: "words", title: "Words" },
  wholeWords: { id: "whole-or-part", title: "The whole thing, or any part of it" },
  cardKeys: { id: "card-keys", title: "Card keys" },
  printingKeys: { id: "printing-keys", title: "Printing keys" },
  numbers: { id: "numbers", title: "Numbers and dates" },
  flags: { id: "flags", title: "Flags" },
  lists: { id: "value-lists", title: "Value lists" },
  mistakes: { id: "mistakes", title: "When a query has a mistake" },
  logic: { id: "logic", title: "Logic" },
  results: { id: "results", title: "Results, sorting, ids" },
};
const sections = Object.values(S);
const textKeys = KEYS.filter((k) => k.kind === "text");
---
<Base title="Search syntax" canonical="/syntax">
  <div class="docs-layout">
    <aside class="docs-nav"><details class="docs-menu" open><summary><span class="eyebrow">On this page</span><span class="current">Sections</span><span class="chevron" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><nav aria-label="On this page"><div class="group">{sections.map((s) => <a href={`#${s.id}`}>{s.title}</a>)}</div></nav></details></aside>
    <article class="docs-content">
  <h1>Search syntax</h1>
  <p>This page is generated from the same table the search engine is built from, so every key listed here works and no other does. Prefer clicking to typing? <a href="/advanced">Advanced search</a> builds these same queries from a form, and shows you the query it built.</p>
  <p class="muted">Each key has one short spelling — the one this page heads its row with — and longer, descriptive ones that mean exactly the same thing: <code>{alias('rarity')}:</code> is <code>rarity:</code>, <code>{alias('cost')}:</code> is <code>mana:</code> and <code>cost:</code>, <code>{alias('life')}:</code> is <code>life:</code>. Keys and values are case-insensitive.</p>

  <details class="syntax-section" id={S.words.id} open>
    <summary><h2>{S.words.title}</h2></summary>
    <p>Bare words match the card <strong>name</strong> (each word, anywhere, case-insensitive): <a href={q("polar")}>polar</a>. <code>!"Polar Bears"</code> is the exact name. Cards whose <em>rules text</em> contains your words appear in a separate group below the name matches, so a bare word never silently mixes the two; use <code>r:</code> to search rules text on purpose.</p>
  </details>

  <details class="syntax-section" id={S.wholeWords.id} open>
    <summary><h2>{S.wholeWords.title}</h2></summary>
    <p>Every key takes two operators, and the difference is the same one everywhere. <code>:</code> ignores where a value begins and ends; <code>=</code> respects it.</p>
    <div class="table-wrap"><table class="reference-table"><thead><tr><th>Syntax</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody>
      <tr><td data-label="Syntax"><a href={q("r:drag")}><code>r:drag</code></a></td><td data-label="Meaning">Those letters anywhere in the rules text, so Dragon counts.</td></tr>
      <tr><td data-label="Syntax"><a href={q("r=drag")}><code>r=drag</code></a></td><td data-label="Meaning">The complete word <em>drag</em>, so Dragon does not.</td></tr>
      <tr><td data-label="Syntax"><a href={q('r="draw a spell"')}><code>r="draw a spell"</code></a></td><td data-label="Meaning">That complete phrase, with a word boundary at each end.</td></tr>
      <tr><td data-label="Syntax"><a href={q("r!=drag")}><code>r!=drag</code></a></td><td data-label="Meaning">Not the complete word <em>drag</em>. Same as <code>-r=drag</code>.</td></tr>
      <tr><td data-label="Syntax"><a href={q("-r:drag")}><code>-r:drag</code></a></td><td data-label="Meaning">Not those letters at all, so no Dragon either.</td></tr>
    </tbody></table></div>
    <p>It is one idea, applied to whatever the key holds. A number is a single value, so <a href={q("m=3")}><code>m=3</code></a> is that value. The elements are a set, so <a href={q("e=water+fire")}><code>e=water+fire</code></a> is that set and no third. Rules text is a sequence of words, so <code>r=</code> is a whole word or a whole phrase. On the keys whose values come from a fixed list, a value has no inside to look at, so <code>t:minion</code> and <code>t=minion</code> ask the same question.</p>
    <p>The whole-word reading applies to every key whose value is free text - {textKeys.map((k, i) => <>{i > 0 && ", "}<code>{k.aliases[0]}</code></>)} - by any of their spellings, so <code>rules=draw</code> is <code>r=draw</code>. A word ends wherever a character is not a letter or a number, so punctuation and line breaks divide words: <a href={q("r=step")}><code>r=step</code></a> finds “step,” at the end of a sentence, and <a href={q("r=force")}><code>r=force</code></a> finds “force-move”. A quoted phrase does not mind how much space sits between its words, so it still matches across the line break a rules box prints.</p>
    <p class="muted"><code>==</code> and <code>!==</code> are accepted as other ways to write <code>=</code> and <code>!=</code>, so a doubled key lands on the operator you meant. And <code>r=polar</code> finds a word <em>inside</em> the text, which is not what <code>!"Polar Bears"</code> does: that is still the entire name and nothing else.</p>
  </details>

  <details class="syntax-section" id={S.cardKeys.id} open>
    <summary><h2>{S.cardKeys.title}</h2></summary>
    <p>These filter cards.</p>
    <div class="table-wrap"><table class="reference-table"><thead><tr><th>Key</th><th>Aliases</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Examples</th></tr></thead><tbody>
      {cardKeys.map((k) => <tr><td data-label="Key"><code>{k.aliases[0]}:</code></td><td data-label="Aliases">{k.aliases.slice(1).map((a) => <code>{a}:</code>)}</td><td data-label="Meaning">{k.doc}{k.values && <span class="muted"> Values: {k.values.join(", ")}.</span>}</td><td data-label="Examples">{k.examples.map((e) => <div><a href={q(e)}><code>{e}</code></a></div>)}</td></tr>)}
    </tbody></table></div>
  </details>

  <details class="syntax-section" id={S.printingKeys.id} open>
    <summary><h2>{S.printingKeys.title}</h2></summary>
    <p><strong>All printing keys and printing flags in a query must be satisfied by one and the same printing of a card.</strong> <code>t:minion s:alpha f:foil</code> means minions with a printing that is both Alpha and foil; <code>or</code> between printing terms is judged printing by printing. Negating a printing term asks for a printing that lacks it: <code>-is:foil</code> is "has a non-foil printing".</p>
    <div class="table-wrap"><table class="reference-table"><thead><tr><th>Key</th><th>Aliases</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Examples</th></tr></thead><tbody>
      {printingKeys.map((k) => <tr><td data-label="Key"><code>{k.aliases[0]}:</code></td><td data-label="Aliases">{k.aliases.slice(1).map((a) => <code>{a}:</code>)}</td><td data-label="Meaning">{k.doc}{k.values && <span class="muted"> Values: {k.values.join(", ")}.</span>}</td><td data-label="Examples">{k.examples.map((e) => <div><a href={q(e)}><code>{e}</code></a></div>)}</td></tr>)}
    </tbody></table></div>
  </details>

  <details class="syntax-section" id={S.numbers.id} open>
    <summary><h2>{S.numbers.title}</h2></summary>
    <p>Every numeric key — {numericKeys.map((k, i) => <>{i > 0 && ", "}<code>{k}</code></>)} — and the date key <code>{dateKey}</code> take <code>:</code> or <code>=</code> (equals), <code>&lt;</code>, <code>&lt;=</code>, <code>&gt;</code>, <code>&gt;=</code>, <code>!=</code>. A numeric key can compare to another: <a href={q("atk>def")}><code>atk&gt;def</code></a>, <a href={q("m>=thr")}><code>m&gt;=thr</code></a>. <code>{alias('threshold')}</code> is the four element thresholds added up, the total a deck has to reach; the element keys ask for one element each, so <a href={q("water>=2 fire:1")}><code>water&gt;=2 fire:1</code></a> is two Water and one Fire. Dates accept YYYY, YYYY-MM or YYYY-MM-DD and compare as ranges: <code>{dateKey}&lt;2024</code> is before 2024, <code>{dateKey}:2024-05</code> is within that month.</p>
  </details>

  <details class="syntax-section" id={S.flags.id} open>
    <summary><h2>{S.flags.title}</h2></summary>
    <div class="table-wrap"><table class="reference-table"><thead><tr><th>Flag</th><th>Scope</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody>
      {IS_FLAGS.map((f) => <tr><td data-label="Flag"><a href={q(`is:${f.name}`)}><code>is:{f.name}</code></a>{f.aliases && <span class="muted"> · {f.aliases.map((a) => <code>is:{a}</code>)}</span>}</td><td data-label="Scope">{f.scope}</td><td data-label="Meaning">{f.doc}</td></tr>)}
      {HAS_FLAGS.map((f) => <tr><td data-label="Flag"><a href={q(`has:${f.name}`)}><code>has:{f.name}</code></a></td><td data-label="Scope">{f.scope}</td><td data-label="Meaning">{f.doc}</td></tr>)}
    </tbody></table></div>
  </details>

  <details class="syntax-section" id={S.lists.id} open>
    <summary><h2>{S.lists.title}</h2></summary>
    <p>Any key whose values are names, rather than free text, accepts several at once: <a href={q("e:water+fire")}><code>e:water+fire</code></a> needs both, <a href={q("e:water,fire")}><code>e:water,fire</code></a> takes either. It works the same for the rest, so <a href={q("t:minion,site")}><code>t:minion,site</code></a> and <a href={q("sub:beast+spirit")}><code>sub:beast+spirit</code></a> read as you would expect. No spaces around the separator, and don't mix <code>+</code> with <code>,</code> in one value: put the alternatives in parentheses instead. A printing key still has to be satisfied by a single printing, so <a href={q("f:foil,rainbow")}><code>f:foil,rainbow</code></a> finds cards printed either way while <code>f:foil+rainbow</code> asks one printing to be both and finds nothing. <code>!=</code> negates the whole list: <a href={q("e!=water,fire")}><code>e!=water,fire</code></a> is neither of them. Elements also take <code>=</code> for the exact set: <a href={q("e=water+fire")}><code>e=water+fire</code></a> is the cards that are those two and nothing more.</p>
  </details>

  <details class="syntax-section" id={S.mistakes.id} open>
    <summary><h2>{S.mistakes.title}</h2></summary>
    <p>Nothing is guessed. An unknown key, a value that is not one of the ones a key accepts (<code>e:wateer</code>, <code>{alias('cost')}:banana</code>), a prefix too short to tell two values apart (<code>{alias('rarity')}:e</code> — Exceptional or Elite?), an unusable operator or a half-finished value list is reported above the results, and the terms that were understood still run — so <code>e:w + f</code> tells you a value list takes no spaces rather than quietly searching for a card named “+”. Mixing <code>+</code> and <code>,</code> in one value is refused for the same reason: <a href={q("(e:water+fire or e:air)")}><code>(e:water+fire or e:air)</code></a> says which grouping you meant.</p>
  </details>

  <details class="syntax-section" id={S.logic.id} open>
    <summary><h2>{S.logic.title}</h2></summary>
    <p>Terms are ANDed; <code>or</code> alternates; parentheses group: <a href={q("t:minion (e:water or e:air) -k:genesis")}><code>t:minion (e:water or e:air) -k:genesis</code></a>. <code>and</code> is accepted and never required. Quote values with spaces: <code>r:"draw a spell"</code>.</p>
    <h3 id="negation">Saying not</h3>
    <p>Three spellings, one meaning — use whichever reads best to you. <code>-</code> in front of a term is the short one and the easiest to type: <a href={q("is:multi-element -t:site -e:air")}><code>is:multi-element -t:site -e:air</code></a> is the multi-element cards that are neither Sites nor Air. <code>not</code> is the same thing in words: <code>not t:site</code>. <code>!=</code> is the same thing on the key, and reads best beside the other comparisons: <a href={q("t!=site")}><code>t!=site</code></a>, <a href={q("m!=3")}><code>m&#33;=3</code></a>. They give identical results — <code>-</code> negates the whole term, which is exactly what <code>!=</code> means.</p>
    <p class="muted">A phone keyboard may offer <code>&#8800;</code> where you want <code>!=</code>; the search does not read it, and will say so rather than looking for a card of that name.</p>
  </details>

  <details class="syntax-section" id={S.results.id} open>
    <summary><h2>{S.results.title}</h2></summary>
    <p><code>unique:</code> {UNITS.map((u) => <code>{u}</code>)} — one row per card (the default, shown with the printing that matched or the default printing), every matching printing, or one per distinct artwork. <code>sort:</code> {SORT_FIELDS.map((s) => <code>{s}</code>)} and <code>order:asc|desc</code> (name ascending by default; dates newest first). Everything lives in the URL, so a search is shareable. A query that is exactly an id (<code>C000230</code>, <code>P000937</code>) or an official slug jumps straight to its page.</p>
  </details>
    </article>
  </div>
</Base>

<script>
  // The section index behaves like the API docs sidebar: on a phone it is
  // a bar that opens on tap, on a wide screen it is the sidebar, always
  // open. The markup ships open, so without JavaScript every screen sees
  // the whole list.
  const menu = document.querySelector<HTMLDetailsElement>("details.docs-menu");
  const narrow = window.matchMedia("(max-width: 740px)");
  const apply = () => { if (menu) menu.open = !narrow.matches; };
  apply();
  narrow.addEventListener("change", apply);

  // Sections fold, and they ship open, so the page still reads whole,
  // prints whole and answers find-in-page without JavaScript. A link into
  // a folded one must still arrive: open the section the hash names, or
  // the one holding the element it names, then scroll to it.
  const reveal = (hash: string) => {
    const id = decodeURIComponent(hash.replace(/^#/, ""));
    if (!id) return;
    const target = document.getElementById(id);
    const section = target?.closest<HTMLDetailsElement>("details.syntax-section");
    if (!section) return;
    section.open = true;
    target!.scrollIntoView();
    if (narrow.matches && menu) menu.open = false;   // Close the drop box behind you.
  };
  reveal(location.hash);
  window.addEventListener("hashchange", () => reveal(location.hash));
</script>
